  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Severe new-onset headache for ~1 day\n- Symptoms: Left-sided, throbbing headache since waking yesterday; comes and goes (fades but doesn't fully resolve); quite intense; photophobia (worse in light/sunlight); no visual changes, no aura, no abnormal smells/tastes; no nausea or vomiting; no fever or infectious prodrome reported; no neck stiffness, no focal weakness, numbness, or sensory changes; improves with lying down; somewhat responsive to paracetamol; plans to take ibuprofen. Onset: sudden on waking yesterday. Duration: ~24 hours. Severity: described as \"quite intense.\" Aggravating: bright light, possibly stress. Alleviating: lying down, rest, paracetamol.\n- Past medical history: none significant reported; no prior recurrent headaches (\"not really, it's not something I've ever had before\").\n- Medications/allergies: Microgynon (combined oral contraceptive for ~6 years); paracetamol taken for this headache; allergic to aspirin (causes nausea). No regular meds otherwise.\n- Family/social history: Mother with hypertension and history of migraines. Patient drinks alcohol moderately (a few drinks at weekend), no smoking, no recreational drugs. Lives with housemates.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: No vitals recorded in transcript; no focal neurological deficits reported on history; no fever reported (not measured). Doctor suggested blood pressure check.\n- Labs/imaging: None mentioned or performed.",
